  "body": "## What changed\n\nThe Fanuc Lathe post can write threads as a two-block G76 cycle.\n\n- New `ThreadCycle` record in the cl crate: start/end, major/minor diameter, lead, height, first-pass and minimum depth, finish allowance, infeed angle, spring passes, pull-out and the moves it covers. It rides the CL stream as `THREAD …` / `THREAD END` around the explicit passes and is saved with the project. Sim and verify still read the passes; posts without the new `onThreadCycle` hook (all but Fanuc Lathe) post them as before.\n- Post option \"Threading: G76 cycle / G32 passes\" (document setting, undoable `SetPostOptions`, saved; a combo under the post picker for Fanuc Lathe). Default G32, so no existing golden changes.\n- With G76 chosen, the post refuses by name when the record is missing or out of range: infeed angle, spring passes, pull-out, depths outside the height, internal thread, bad start/end, no lead or no length.\n- Decisions (RECOMMENDED.md): the kernel plunges straight in, so infeed angle 0 and first = minimum depth = the kernel's step, and G76 cuts the same depth sequence. G76 needs ≥ 1 finishing pass, so the control makes one extra full-depth pass, no deeper than the verified root. Only straight external threads are supported (no taper word).\n\n## Golden (`tests/golden/nc/fanuc-lathe/thread_g76.nc`)\n\n```\nG00 X12.1 Z8\nG76 P010000 Q400 R0\nG76 X9.6 Z14 P1200 Q400 F2\nG00 X12.1 Z24\n```\nThe full in-app post path, gate included, matches byte for byte.\n\n## Readings\n\nmotion turning_thread 18, turning_thread_cycle 1; post_engine 30; app turning_thread 8, lathe_post 7, lathe_post_refusals 5, lathe_post_g76 5, conformance 6, golden_cl 23, posting 17; clippy on cl, motion, post, app and ui is clean.\n\nChangelog: Fanuc Lathe post can write threads as a two-block G76 cycle (new post option \"Threading: G76 cycle / G32 passes\", default G32), from a thread-cycle record the threading toolpath now carries\n\nFixes #34\n\n🤖 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
